	Pretty much all of the Norse deities have "war" in their
portfolios. I mean, even Freya, goddess of love and kittens, is in
charge of collecting the souls of the dead from the battlefield (she
really ought to be the Valkyrie Quest Leader).

	But Thor and Tyr are definitely distinct deities... Thor's got a
red beard and a hammer with which we're all familiar, and is the
thunder-god, blesses marriages with Mjollnir (there's probably something
Freudian going on there), and is probably the AEsir's best fighter. He
got Thursday named after him.

	Tyr has only one hand, and is the god of justice. These are
related... it seems that Tyr, to get Fenris to agree to be bound with
Gleipnir (a chain forged from, IIRC, the noise of a cat's footfalls, the
breath of a fish, the beard of a maiden, and the roots of a mountain),
promised that he would place his hand between Fenris's jaws, as surety
that they would unbind him if he couldn't break it. They bound him, he
couldn't get free, and Tyr allowed Fenris to bite off his hand. This is
a guy who takes oaths seriously. He got Tuesday named after him... he's
called "Tiw" in Old English.

	Wednesday is Woden's Day, who we've all met as "Odin". Odin is
probably more the "war god" than anyone else. He wields the spear
Gungnir, which never misses, rides the eight-legged warhorse Sleipnir
(whose parents were Loki and a jotun stallion... and yes, that makes
Loki the *mother*), and splits the warrior souls retrieved by the
valkyries with Freya (which implies that valks should be required to be
neutral, followers of Odin). In an interesting twist, he's also the god
of knowledge and wisdom... he invented writing and poetry, and
sacrificed his eye and hanged himself from the Tree of Life, not for
martial prowess, but for *knowledge*.

